00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760 align:start position:0%
 
as you know we are here in Manchester at  the Conservative Party conference but  this city is controlled by a Labour  mayor and that mayor is Andy Burnham and  he joins us now thank you very much for  being on the program with us and mr.  Burnham you're welcome of course we're  speaking we're speaking at the  Conservative Party conference in  Manchester now something that according  to marketing Manchester is worth around  32 million pounds to the local economy  this week the Labour Party and other  opposition parties voted to not allow a  short recess for this conference which  of course means that as less MPs  spending less time in Manchester  spending less money was that mistake I  don't think so because I think the  government brought the wrong proposal  Sophie to the Commons they basically  said we want our conference in full you  know the complete conference right  through Wednesday through Prime  Minister's Questions if they've gone  with a proposal that said we want the  same conference that Labour had I you  know from Sunday till Tuesday I think  that would have got through and I was  pushing for that behind behind the  scenes so to be honest with you I just  think the government really got it wrong  after last week's Supreme Court  judgement and they lost 11 nil there and  then the Prime Minister goes into the  Commons all guns blazing the next day  you know it should have been more  humility I would have said and a  different tone completely and they  should have said you know we'll do the  same as Labour had you know we'll have a  three day conference and I think that  would have been a a fair compromise just  to kind of say oh no we want the full  thing without any change and the PM lots  of promises questions I don't think that  was a sensible move I just say so we  were laid on a traditional Manchester  welcome today there's one hour later  this afternoon where it doesn't rain so  that's a very Manchester yes well we  were looking forward to that short pause  and the downpours that we've been  experiencing so far that sound something  definitely to look forward to and you  mentioned in your answer and some of the  issues shall we say overshadowing the  Conservative Party confidence one of  them of course these persistent  allegations about Boris Johnson's time  as mayor now you of course on there of a  big city you know about these things  are you concerned about the allegations  around Jennifer our Curie the  businesswoman where there are claims of  course an investigation going on into  those claims that Boris Johnson gave her  privileged access well yes I mean they  are serious allegations I don't know if  they are true or not but of course they  they have to be fully investigated and  we all accept that in the roles that we  do be that as mayor or as minister or as  Prime Minister that comes with the job  so I think it's right that they're being  fully investigated but you know I'm not  going to come on this morning and say  that you know I disagree with everything  that he's done so far what the prime  minister said about northern transport  is promising giving us control of our  railways promising to invest in our our  buses you know these things are positive  and I think having been a mayor and now  being Prime Minister he sees the  challenges that cities like Manchester  are facing but you know what I would say  that the main point I would say this  morning at Sophie is this  take-no-prisoners approach to brexit I  think is unnerving people and that led  to the supercharged atmosphere in the  Commons the other day it was a wrong  approach completely when we had that  Supreme Court judgement you know it's  ratcheting up the atmosphere and I don't  think that's going to help anybody so if  he were ever to listen to me and if I  were to give him some advice you know  last week was a low point this  conference needs to be a turning point  we need to see a different tone I think  the the government needs to start  talking about issues that affect people  the north of England they made a lot of  promises to us Sophie but last week the  government issued figures themselves  that said out of the 100 most deprived  areas neighborhoods in the country 82 of  them are in the north of England  you know hs2 and promises of that  doesn't solve that level of poverty what  will solve it is if they start giving  money to northern councils  again and reversing all of the cuts that  they've made I mean these are the kind  of issues that need to be spoken about  as well as a different toll  on brexit talking about the tone and  it's clear that you have concerns about  some of the language and that we're  seeing in the debates about politics of  a minute I was quite interested in some  things that you said at a conference  this week you said that social media has  completely disrupted our political  system which can't cope do you think  that's a form of radicalization almost  well I certainly felt the change Sophie  I was in Parliament eight years before  social media and eight years after and  you know the second half was was like a  completely different job that that I was  doing I think what it's done is it's  broken down the the collective ways of  Parliament you know Parliament has to  work on those collective principles  through the Tea Room and the PLP in the  1922 committee I think social media has  made people become more individual but I  also feel when I watch Parliament's at  the moment some people are kind of  thinking about the clip that will play  on social media when they're making  their speech and they're not making it  in the let's say the olds more  considered way they're doing it in a way  that's that's playing to a certain  position on social media and yeah I  think it's it's kind of ratcheted up the  whole atmosphere and not for the better  and then there are some ways in which  social media to improve politics in  terms of bottom-up change and people  having a stronger voice but in my view  it did begin to damage Parliament quite  significantly  it was always quite a dysfunctional  world as we all know but social media  has made it much more so and I think now  this is partly why Parliament is  struggling to make sense of what's going  on everybody's in these splintered  positions and you know we're struggling  to find compromise in these quite  polarized times and as I say all  politicians need to take a step back  here moderate the language think about  compromise again they seem to have lost  the art of compromise and I think social  media is is partly responsible for that  too now it's an Conservative Party  conference this weekend I just came to  ask your thoughts about Labour's  conference last weekend where it backed  a brexit position which was to win an  election  trying to go share a deal and  put it back as a second referendum and  it wouldn't confirm how it would  campaign in that second referendum do  you think labour needs to unequivocally  come out full remain no because I think  that would be quite damaging the Liberal  Democrats did that and it appeared to me  that that was a very arrogant move  certainly to the the people who voted  leave and I think that move that they  that they made probably further damaged  democracy further polarized people so I  think labour actually has been the only  party trying to bring people together  across the divide personally I would  have encouraged them to take a slightly  different approach last week  the focus should be on stopping No Deal  brexit and then getting a sensible deal  through that is where I think the  national interest still lies if anyone  was to listen to me I would recommend  now that there needs to be compromise on  all sides and that we should leave the  European Union on a Norway style deal  effectively as an interim position so  that we can kind of honor the referendum  but then carry on debating after we've  left in the years to come what our end  position with Europe will be I mean that  would be a much better way to do things  rather than talking about second  referendums or revoking article 50 I  mean that is kind of a think further  fueling that the sort of distrust of  politics that we're all feeling right  now and as I say politicians have lost  the art of compromise and then they need  to rediscover it pretty quickly because  the only way we get through this is  through compromise on all sides just  finally there's been quite a lot of  speculation after they've a conference  about leadership succession planning  whether or not Emily Thornberry or cast  armor could be potential leaders of the  future now you've had a crack at it  before could you be tempted to run again  I mean the current Prime Minister's a  former mayor well you mentioned my to  twice actually yet Sophie not just once  and you know I mean I lost and I know  many ways why I lost people were looking  for a change and not the sort of  continuity that we'd seen through  the labor governments of the last the  last decade so to be honest I kind of  feel a bit liberated being out of the  the dysfunctional world of Westminster  it never worked for me individually very  much no you kind of have to go with its  strange ways but it didn't work for me  personally but I don't think it works  for the north of England either you know  it is inherently a London centric system  and I think the problem with our  politics over many years has been the  the idea that Westminster is the only  show in town you know we've got a very  over centralized political system here  and I think that that system Westminster  has created the north/south divide it's  never looked after the north in the same  way that it's looked after London and  the south and so you know rather than me  sort of sitting looking like I'm biding  my time here plotting a return to  Westminster actually what I'm trying to  do is is build up English devolution  because I think this is a big part of  the answer to the to the whole the  country finds itself in at the moment  give more power to people put it in  their own hands more equal funding right  across the country that will build a  healthier politics it maybe than the one  we've got right now

